02:06.180 --> 02:10.100
Something that you'll notice is that we're not actually doing anything special

02:10.100 --> 02:12.680
as far as calling the data that's inside of the app, right?

02:12.740 --> 02:13.960
We have this characterList array.

02:14.620 --> 02:18.740
And all we're doing is saying, as you can see here, characterList, character.

02:18.980 --> 02:20.500
We're basically naming things as we go.

02:20.880 --> 02:24.600
And it's because Vue is basically trying to do that step of like abstracting away

02:24.600 --> 02:26.240
the things that you would normally have to define.

02:26.640 --> 02:28.480
So if this were JavaScript, you might have to be like,

02:28.480 --> 02:33.100
oh, I have to call the app.data.characterList to be like, I'm calling characterList.

02:33.480 --> 02:37.600
But Vue knows that basically there are conventions in the way you kind of call things.

02:37.880 --> 02:39.860
So rather than having you explicitly define everything,

02:40.220 --> 02:42.920
it is trying to basically help abstract that away.

02:43.480 --> 02:46.080
But in the future, for those who want to be more explicit,

02:46.220 --> 02:49.060
there are ways to kind of namespace that out a little bit more.

02:49.360 --> 02:51.060
So we'll get into that later on.

02:52.080 --> 02:52.360
Cool.

