02:54.420 --> 02:58.000
I mean, you could almost say that characterList is like a global, in a sense,

02:58.000 --> 02:59.360
are global to the app?

03:00.140 --> 03:00.580
Yes.

03:00.640 --> 03:01.480
At this point, yeah.

03:01.540 --> 03:02.960
Especially once we start getting into components,

03:03.160 --> 03:05.380
because components obviously is a big part of frameworks these days,

03:05.820 --> 03:09.940
that will definitely, characterList will become, well, hold on.

03:10.060 --> 03:11.060
Let me backtrack.

03:12.460 --> 03:15.320
So your question here is regarding whether or not characterList

03:15.320 --> 03:16.880
becomes global to the entire app.

03:17.280 --> 03:24.760
And so it is global to the context in that specific rendering context.

03:24.760 --> 03:29.580
So in the case of this page, right, it has the characterList, it can show it.

03:29.940 --> 03:32.380
When we start getting into components, it will have its own scope.

03:32.820 --> 03:34.780
And it will be separate from the parent.

03:34.920 --> 03:38.640
So in that regard, you cannot expect characterList to just show up in any context.

03:39.460 --> 03:43.060
So in Vue, it tries to basically try to optimize for performance.

03:45.480 --> 03:47.920
There's a performance impact when it comes to making things global.

03:48.040 --> 03:51.920
But more importantly, you have namespace polluting that often happens.

03:52.400 --> 03:57.040
And so Vue tries to protect you from that by forcing you to be explicit with what you want

03:57.040 --> 03:58.660
to be shared and what you want to be made global.

03:58.860 --> 04:01.900
So there are ways to make global variables in Vue.

04:02.080 --> 04:04.600
But I would say most of the time, you're probably not doing that.

04:05.080 --> 04:07.280
And there are other ways you're trying to share data.

04:07.440 --> 04:11.680
So even in the instance of, say, the use case regarding, like, a user being logged in,

04:11.900 --> 04:13.880
that's something that impacts a lot of different components.

04:14.500 --> 04:17.600
And so in the past, the first thing you might be, like, tempted to do is say,

04:17.900 --> 04:20.760
well, I'm just going to make a global variable that says, hey, is the user logged in?

04:20.980 --> 04:24.420
Because, I don't know, call it 30% of our code base uses it.

04:24.420 --> 04:27.400
But that also means 70% of your code base doesn't use it.

04:27.520 --> 04:30.560
And now it has this user logged in state that it has to either worry about

04:30.560 --> 04:32.360
or it might conflict for some other reason.

04:32.360 --> 04:36.500
And so Vue 3 in particular, especially with the Composition API and Composables,

04:36.780 --> 04:39.060
which I promise we'll cover in this workshop,

04:39.620 --> 04:42.580
allows you to then segment that to be very explicit that, like,

04:42.780 --> 04:45.760
on the 30% that care about user logged in, great, drop it in.

04:46.000 --> 04:48.520
Otherwise, you don't even have to worry about it.

04:49.040 --> 04:51.260
And so we'll definitely be talking about that.

